solution_md:
(a) For  $g(x)$  to exist, for all  $t \in [10, x]$ ,  $f(t)= \log_{10}(\log_{10}(t^2-1000t+10^{1000}))$  must exist.
This implies  $\log_{10}(t^2-1000t+10^{1000}) > 0$  as argument of log has to be positive. $\implies t^2-1000t+10^{1000} > 10$ Note that  $t^2-1000t$  has a positive leading cofficient. This means a minima exists. The minima occurs at  $t = 500$ . $\implies t^2-100t+10^{1000} \geq 500^2 - 100*500+ 10^{1000} = 10^{1000}-2500 > 10$ Thus, for all  $t$ ,  $\log_{10}(\log_{10}(t^2-1000t+10^{1000}))$  exists. Thus, domain of  $g(x)$  is  $\mathbb{R}$ .

(b) \begin{align*} 
g(1000) &= \int_{10}^{1000} \log_{10}(\log_{10}(t^2-1000t+10^{1000})) dt
&= \int_{10}^{500} f(t) dt + \int_{500}^{1000} f(t) dt
&= g(500) + \int_{500}^{1000} f(t) dt
\end{align*}
Using the fact that  $t^2-1000t+10^{1000}$  decreases from  $[10,500]$ ,  $490*f(10) \geq g(500) \geq 490*f(500)$ .
As  $10^2-1000*10+10^{1000} \approx 10^{1000}$  and  $500^2 - 500*1000 + 10^{1000} \approx 10^{1000}$ .
Note that  $\log_{10}(\log_{10}(1000)) = 3$  $\implies 1470 \geq g(500) \geq 1470 \implies g(500) \approx 1470$ .
Similerly,  $\int_{500}^{1000} f(t) dt \approx 500*3 = 1500$ .
Thus,  $g(1000) = 2970$ .
(c) Slope of  $g(x) = g'(x) = f(x)$ The value of  $f(x)$  increases as value of  $x^2-1000x+10^{1000}$  increases. As it achives minimum on  $500$ , that is is decreasing from  $[10,500]$  and increasing from  $[500, 1000]$ .
We will compare  $1000^2 - 1000*1000 + 10^{1000}$  and  $10^2 - 1000*10 + 10^{1000}$ , to find that  $10^{1000} > 10^{1000}-9900$ .
Thus, slip is maximized at  $x=1000$ .
(d) Slope is minimized at  $x=500$ .
(e) It is obvious that plugging in  $x = \infty$  will give  $\frac{\infty}{\infty}$  which is indeterminate. Hence, using L-Hopital,
\begin{align*} 
\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\ln(x)}{g(x)}
&= \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\frac{1}{x}}{g'(x)}
&= \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\frac{1}{x}}{g'(x)}
\end{align*}
We can evauate this limit as  $\frac{0}{\infty}$  which is not indeterminate and indeed approaches  $0$ .

