When  $p=3,$  we have  $4p-3=9$  is a square number. Now consider the case when  $p> 3.$ We have  $\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
n|p - 1
p|(n - 1)({n^2} + n + 1)
\end{array} \right. ,$  let  $d=\gcd (n-1,n^2+n+1)\Rightarrow n\equiv 1(\bmod d)\Rightarrow d|3\Rightarrow d\in \{ 1;3 \}$  $\bullet$  Case 1.  $d=3\Rightarrow p|\dfrac{n-1}{3}.\dfrac{n^2+n+1}{3},$  note that  $\gcd \left( {\dfrac{{n - 1}}{3},\dfrac{{{n^2} + n + 1}}{3}} \right) = 1\Rightarrow \left[ \begin{array}{l}
p|\dfrac{{n - 1}}{3}\Rightarrow n+1\le p \le \dfrac{n-1}{3} (!)
p|\dfrac{{{n^2} + n + 1}}{3}
\end{array} \right.$  $\Rightarrow p|n^2+n+1$  $\bullet$  Case 2.  $d=1\Rightarrow \left[ \begin{array}{l}
p|n - 1 \Rightarrow n + 1 \le p \le n - 1(!)
p|{n^2} + n + 1
\end{array} \right. \Rightarrow p|n^2+n+1$ So we always have  $p|n^2+n+1\Rightarrow n^2+n+1=pk (k\in \mathbb{Z}^+),$  note that  $p\equiv 1(\bmod n)\Rightarrow k\equiv 1(\bmod n),$  if  $k>1,$ We let  $\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
p = an + 1
k = bn + 1
\end{array} \right.\left( {a,b \in \mathbb{Z}^+} \right) \Rightarrow n^2+n+1=(an+1)(bn+1)\Leftrightarrow n+1=abn+a+b\ge n+2 (!)$ Hence  $k$  must be equal to  $1\Rightarrow p=n^2+n+1\Rightarrow 4p-3=(2n+1)^2.$ Conclusion:  $4p-3$  is a square number.
