We will prove that \( n = 2^k - 1 \), where \( k \) is a positive integer, satisfies the given condition. 
<details><summary>Lemma P</summary>Consider a circle with \( 2^{k+1} \), $ (k\in \mathbb{Z}^+)$  lamps, where exactly \( 2^k \) consecutive lamps are turned on, and the rest are off.  
Then, in \( 2^{k-1} \) seconds, all lamps will be turned on.
Example for  $k=3:$ [asy]
size(200);
int k = 3; 
int n = 2^(k+1);
int on = 2^k;

pair P[];
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    P.push(dir(360*j/n));
}

// Dibujar lámparas
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    if (j < on) {
        fill(circle(P[j], 0.1), black); // Encendidas
    } else {
        draw(circle(P[j], 0.1)); // Apagadas
    }
}
[/asy]</details>
<details><summary>Proof of Lemma P</summary>We proceed by induction on \( k \).   $\textbf{Base Case:}$  It is evident for \( k = 1 \).   $\textbf{Inductive Hypothesis:}$  We assume that **Lemma P** holds for all \( k \leq L \), where \( L \in \mathbb{Z}^+ \).   $\textbf{Inductive Step: \( k = L+1 \)}$   
After one second, we obtain the following configuration: two consecutive lamps are on, and their two opposite lamps are also on, while all other lamps are off.  
[asy]
size(200);
int k = 4; // Example for k=4
int n = 2^(k+1);
pair P[];
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    P.push(dir(360*j/n));
}
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    if (j < 2 || j >= n/2 && j < n/2 + 2) {
        fill(circle(P[j], 0.1), black); // On lamps
    } else {
        draw(circle(P[j], 0.1)); // Off lamps
    }
}
[/asy]
We proceed using the following algorithm for \( 1 \leq i \leq L-1 \):  
- Initially, we have \( 2^i \) consecutive lamps turned on, as well as their opposite \( 2^i \) consecutive lamps, while the rest are off.  
- For each region with \( 2^i \) consecutive on-lamps, we select the \( 2^{i-1} \) adjacent off-lamps on both sides (i.e., \( 2^{i-1} \) on each extreme).  
- It is easy to see that all selected lamps are distinct.  
- Now, we have two separate groups of \( 2^{i+1} \) lamps, each in the form of **Lemma P** for \( k = i+1 \), which, by the inductive hypothesis, holds true.  
- Thus, we can ensure that all these lamps turn on, except for the remaining off-lamps, in \( 2^{i-1} \) steps.  
- If not all lamps are on, we continue applying the algorithm; otherwise, for \( i = L-1 \), we terminate the algorithm.  

Now, we achieve the desired result in:  
\[
1 + 2^0 + 2^1 + 2^2 + \dots + 2^{L-2} = 2^{L-1}
\]  
which completes the induction $_\blacksquare$</details>
<details><summary>Lemma 1</summary>Consider \( 2^k - 1 \) lamps arranged in a circle (\( k \geq 3 \)), where:  
- The first \( 2^i \) consecutive lamps are on.  
- The next \( 2^{k-1} - 2^i - 1 \) lamps are off.  
- The following \( 2^i \) consecutive lamps are on.  
- The final \( 2^{k-1} - 2^i \) lamps are off.  

Here is an example for \( k = 5 \), \( i = 2 \):
[asy]
size(250);
int k = 5; 
int i = 2; 
int n = 2^k - 1;

int on1 = 2^i;
int off1 = 2^(k-1) - 2^i - 1;
int on2 = 2^i;
int off2 = 2^(k-1) - 2^i;

pair P[];
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    P[j] = rotate(360*j/n) * (1,0);
}

for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    draw(circle(P[j], 0.1));
    if (j < on1 || (j >= on1 + off1 && j < on1 + off1 + on2)) {
        fill(circle(P[j], 0.1), black);
    }
}
[/asy]

Then, after \( 2^{i-1} \) seconds, we reach the same configuration but with \( i+1 \), for all \( i \in \{1,2,\dots,k-2\} \).  

In the case \( i = k-2 \), after \( 2^{k-3} \) steps, all lamps will be turned on.</details>
<details><summary>Lemma 2</summary>Consider \( 2^k - 1 \) lamps arranged in a circle, where \( 2^{k-1} - 1 \) consecutive lamps are on, and the rest are off.  
Then, after \( 2^{k-1} \) seconds, all the lamps will be turned off.
For example, for  $k=5:$ [asy]
size(250);
int k = 5; // Puedes cambiar el valor de k
int n = 2^k - 1;
int off = 2^(k-1);

pair P[];
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    P[j] = rotate(360*j/n) * (1,0);
}

// Dibujar todas las lámparas
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    draw(circle(P[j], 0.1));
}

// Encender las lámparas fuera de la zona apagada
for (int j = off; j < n; ++j) {
    fill(circle(P[j], 0.1), black);
}
[/asy]</details>
<details><summary>Proof of Lemmas 1 and 2</summary>We will prove them by induction. $\textbf{Base Cases:} $ It is evident that **Lemma 2** holds for \( k = 1, 2 \).  
Similarly, **Lemma 1** is clearly true for \( k = 3 \) (it is only necessary to verify the case \( i = 1 \)). $\textbf{Inductive Hypothesis:}$ Assume that **Lemma 2** holds for all \( k \leq L \), and **Lemma 1** holds for all \( k \leq L+1 \), where \( L \geq 2 \) is an integer. $\textbf{Inductive Step:} $ We now prove **Lemma 2** for \( k = L+1 \):  
After one step, the configuration transitions to that of **Lemma 1** with \( k = L+1 \) and \( i = 1 \).  
By the inductive hypothesis, **Lemma 1** is true for \( k = L+1 \), so we need  
\[
2^{1-1} + 2^{2-1} + \dots + 2^{k-1} = 2^k - 1
\]
seconds to reach a state where all lamps are on.  
After one more second, all lamps will be off, proving **Lemma 2** for \( k = L+1 \).
Now, let us analyze**Lemma 1** for \( k = L+2 \), with \( i \in \{1,2,\dots,L\} \):  
We select one of the two regions containing \( 2^i \) consecutive lamps that are turned on.  
Observe that if we choose the \( 2^{i-1} \) adjacent off-lamps from the region containing \( 2^{L+1} - 2^i \) off-lamps and the \( 2^{i-1} \) adjacent off-lamps from the region containing \( 2^{L+1} - 2^i - 1 \) off-lamps, we obtain a total of \( 2^{i+1} - 1 \) lamps.  
We apply the same process symmetrically to the other region containing \( 2^i \) on-lamps. $\textbf{Case 1:}$  \( i \leq L-1 \)  
Clearly,  
\[
2^{L+1} - 2^i - 1 \geq 2^{i-1} + 2^{i-1} \quad \text{for all } i \leq L-1
\]
and  
\[
2^{L+1} - 2^i \geq 2^{i-1} + 2^{i-1} = 2^i
\]
since \( i \leq (L+2)-2 = L \).  

Now, note that the selected sets of lamps are disjoint.  
Each of these sets corresponds to the **Lemma P** configuration for \( k = i \), which requires \( 2^{i-1} \) seconds to turn all its lamps on.  
Furthermore, the remaining lamps will stay off, since the number of off-lamps decreases by 1 at each step, and we have precisely chosen \( 2^{i-1} \) from each off-region.  
This proves **Lemma 1** in this case. $\textbf{Case 2:}$  \( i = L \)  
Clearly,  
\[
2^{L+1} - 2^i - 1 = 2^{i-1} + 2^{i-1} - 1
\]
and  
\[
2^{L+1} - 2^i = 2^{i-1} + 2^{i-1} = 2^i.
\]  
This implies that the selected sets of lamps intersect at exactly one lamp.  

Applying **Lemma P** to each set, we observe that, similarly to the previous case, all the lamps will be turned on in \( 2^{i-1} \) seconds.  
Now, we analyze the intersection (the single lamp that might still be off).  
In the second-to-last step, both adjacent lamps to this intersection are off, which means that this lamp will turn on.  
Thus, after \( 2^{i-1} \) seconds, all lamps are turned on, as desired $_\blacksquare$</details>
<details><summary>Final Step</summary>We will prove by induction on \( k \) that all lamps can be turned off in \( 2^k \) seconds.   $\textbf{Base Cases:}$  The statement is trivially true for \( k = 1,2 \).   $\textbf{Inductive Hypothesis:}$  Assume the statement holds for all \( k \leq L \), where \( L \geq 2 \).   $\textbf{Inductive Step:}$  Consider \( k = L+1 \).  

Initially, there is a single illuminated lamp, while all others are turned off.  
We select the \( 2^{L}-1 \) lamps adjacent to the illuminated lamp, both to the left and right.  

[asy]
size(200);
int L = 4; // You may adjust L as needed
int k = L + 1;
int n = 2^k; 

pair P[];
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    P.push(dir(360*j/n));
}

// Draw lamps
for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    draw(circle(P[j], 0.08));
}

// Highlight the initially illuminated central lamp
fill(circle(P[0], 0.08), black);

// Select the 2^{L-1}-1 lamps on both sides
int range = 2^(L-1) - 1;
for (int j = 1; j <= range; ++j) {
    draw(circle(P[j], 0.1), red);
    draw(circle(P[n-j], 0.1), red);
}

[/asy]

By the  $\textbf{Inductive Hypothesis}$ , applying the case \( k = L \) to the \( 2(2^{L-1}-1)+1 = 2^L - 1 \) selected lamps, we obtain that in \( 2^L \) seconds, \( 2^L - 1 \) lamps will be illuminated while the remaining ones stay off.  
By **Lemma 2**, within another \( 2^L \) seconds, all lamps will be turned off.  
Thus, in \( 2^{L+1} \) seconds, all lamps are turned off, as required.</details>

