Assume for the sake of contradiction that  $a_m\le 0$ . Then we must have  $a_1\le a_2\le \cdots\le a_m\le 0$ . Observe that
\begin{align*}
0 &\ge a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_m 
a_n+a_{n-1}+\cdots+a_{m+1} &\ge a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{n-1}+a_n. 
\end{align*}
Since  $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n>0$   we get   $$ (a_n+a_{n-1}+\cdots+a_{m+1})^2\ge (a_1^2+a_2^2+\cdots+a_n^2)=\dfrac{n}{2}(a_1^2+a_2^2+\cdots+a_n^2). $$  By Titu's Lemma we have  $(n-m)(a_n^2+a_{n-1}^2\cdots+a_{m+1}^2)\ge (a_n+a_{n-1}+\cdots+a_{m+1})^2$ . Hence we obtain
\begin{align*}
(n-m)(a_n^2+a_{n-1}^2+\cdots+a_{m+1}^2) &\ge \dfrac{n}{2}(a_n^2+a_{n-1}^2+\cdots +a_1^2) 
\left(\dfrac{n}{2}-m\right)(a_n^2+a_{n-1}^2+\cdots+a_{m+1}^2) &\ge \dfrac{n}{2}(a_m^2+a_{m-1}^2+\cdots+a_1^2)\ge0.
\end{align*}

But we have reached a contradiction since  $\left(\dfrac{n}{2}-m\right)(a_n^2+a_{n-1}^2+\cdots+a_{m+1}^2)<0$   as  $m=\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1$ . Therefore we must have  $a_m>0$  as desired.

