Diagram is harder than the question itself. You have to take base  $BC$ . Here is a solution with just length chase. We shall prove  $\triangle ABE$  is equilateral. Pretty decent for a marker.

*Solution:* Let  $\angle DAE = \angle EAB = x$ . Since  $DA \parallel BC$ , we have  $\angle DAE = \angle AEB = x$  giving us  $\triangle ABE$  isosceles with  $\overline{AB}= \overline{BE}$ . In any isosceles triangle, it is well known that  $MP \parallel AE$  and if  $Q$  is the touch point at  $AE$ , then  $Q$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AE}$ . By basic proportionality theorem, if  $\overline{BM} = \overline{BP} = d$  then  $\overline{AP} = \overline{ME} = dk$  for some constant  $k$ . Now due to  $\triangle BMP \sim \triangle BEA$  we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{x} &= \frac{d+dk}{2dk} 
d+dk &= 2x
\end{align*}
Solving this system of equations and eliminating  $k$  we get  $d = x$ . This finally gives  $\triangle ABE$  equilateral. Therefore,  $\angle BAE = \angle DAE = 60^\circ$  and then we are done.

