Given  $f:\mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R} = (m^2-12)x^4 - 8x^2 - 4$ , if  $m^2-12 \le 0$  we have  $f(x) < 0$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R} \Rightarrow$  no real roots. If  $m^2-12 > 0$ , then we have: $f'(x) = 4(m^2-12)x^3 - 16x = x[4(m^2-12)x^2 - 16] = 0  \Rightarrow x = 0, \pm \frac{2}{\sqrt{m^2-12}}$ ; $f''(x) = 12(m^2-12)x^2 - 16  \Rightarrow f''(0) = -16 < 0$  (a local maximum);  $f''(\pm 2/\sqrt{m^2-12}) = 44 > 0$  (a local minimum);

Therefore,  $f(0) = -4$  and  $f(\pm 2/\sqrt{m^2-12}) = -\frac{16}{m^2-12} - 4$ , which are all negative quantities.  But  $f(x) \rightarrow \infty$  as  $x \rightarrow \pm \infty$ , which means there are  $2$  real roots in this case.

The greatest value of  $m$  that guarantees no real roots for  $f(x)$  is  $2\sqrt{3} \Rightarrow p = 2, q =3$  and  $p+q=5$ .  Choice B is our answer.

Draw right isosceles  $\Delta PQR$  in the  $xy-$ plane such that  $P(0,0); Q(\sqrt{2}s, 0); R(s/\sqrt{2}, s/\sqrt{2})$ .  Take the internal point  $A(x,y)$  so that we obtain: $x^2 + y^2 = 11^2$  (i); $(x-\sqrt{2}s)^2 +y^2 = 7^2$  (ii); $(x-s/\sqrt{2})^2+(y-s/\sqrt{2})^2 = 6^2$  (iii).

Expanding (ii) out yields: $x^2 + y^2 -2\sqrt{2}sx + 2s^2 =7^2 \Rightarrow 11^2 - 7^2 + 2s^2 = 2\sqrt{2}sx \Rightarrow \frac{36+s^2}{\sqrt{2}s} = x$  (iv).  

Also, expanding (iii) out yields: $x^2 + y^2  - \sqrt{2}s(x+y) + s^2 = 6^2 \Rightarrow 11^2 -6^2 - (36+s^2) + s^2 =\sqrt{2}sy \Rightarrow \frac{49}{\sqrt{2}s} = y$  (v).

Finally, we substitute (iv) and (v) into (i) to compute the side length  $s$ : $11^2 = \left(\frac{36+s^2}{\sqrt{2}s} \right)^{2} + \left(\frac{49}{\sqrt{2}s} \right)^{2}$ ;

or  $121 = \frac{(36+s^2)^2}{2s^2} + \frac{49^2}{2s^2}$ ;

or  $242s^2 = s^4 + 72s^2 + 36^2 + 49^2$ ;

or  $0 = s^4 - 170s^2 + 3697$ ;

or  $s^2 = \frac{170 \pm \sqrt{170^2-4(1)(3697)}}{2} = \frac{170 + \sqrt{14112}}{2} = \frac{170+84\sqrt{2}}{2} = 85+42\sqrt{2}$ ;

or  $s=\sqrt{85+42\sqrt{2}}$ .

Hence,  $a=85, b = 42 \Rightarrow a+b =$  $\fbox{127}$ .

