Let  $S = AK \cap PQ$ . Since the incircle of  $\triangle ABC$  is the circumcircle of  $\triangle KLM$ ,  $KS$  is the  $K$ -symmedian of  $\triangle KLM$ . Take any point  $T$  on the internal angle bisector of  $\angle LKM$ . Reflect  $\overline{KL}, \overline{KM}$  over  $KT$  to form  $\overline{KL'},\overline{KM'}$ .  Let  $S'$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{L'M'}$ . Since  $KS$  was the  $K$ -symmedian of  $\triangle KLM$ ,  $KS$  is the  $K$ -median of  $\triangle KL'M'$ . Now since  $L',M'$  lie on  $KP,KQ$  respectively, and  $KS$  is also the median of  $\triangle KPQ$  (as  $APKQ$  is a parallelogram), we have  $PQ \parallel L'M' \implies \triangle KLM \sim \triangle KPQ$ . So  $\angle PQK = \angle LMK = \angle LKC$  (alternate segment theorem). So  $PQ \parallel BC$ . Since  $S$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AK}$ , and  $PS \parallel BK$ , so  $PQ$  bisects  $AB$  and similarly  $AC$ .  $\blacksquare$

