Treating the equation as a quadratic in  $a$ ,
\[ a=\frac{-3b-2p\pm\sqrt{9b^2+4pb}}{2}, \] so  $9b^2+4bp=m^2$  for some integer  $m$ .  Then $81b^2+36bp+4p^2=9m^2+4p^2$ , so $(9b+2p)^2=(3m)^2+(2p)^2$ .  Hence  $\{3m,2p,9b+2p\}$  is a solution to the Pythagorean equation, so  $\{3m,2p\}=\{u^2-v^2,2uv\}$  for some integers  $u$ ,  $v$ , with  $u^2+v^2=\pm(9b+2p)$ .

In fact,  $2p\equiv 2 \text{ (mod 4) }\not\equiv u^2-v^2$ , so  $2p=2uv$  and  $u=\pm p$ , $v=\pm 1$ .  Hence
\begin{eqnarray*} m&=&\frac{u^2-v^2}{3}=\frac{p^2-1}{3} b&=&\frac{\pm(u^2+v^2)-2p}{9}=\frac{\pm(p^2+1)-2p}{9}=\left(\frac{p-1}{3}\right)^2 \text{ or } -\left(\frac{p+1}{3}\right)^2 \end{eqnarray*}
If the former, 
\begin{eqnarray*} a&=&\frac{-3\frac{p^2+1-2p}{9}-2p\pm\frac{p^2-1}{3}}{2}=\frac{-1-2p}{3} \text{ or } \frac{-p^2-2p}{3} \end{eqnarray*}
If the latter, 
\begin{eqnarray*} a&=&\frac{3\frac{p^2+1+2p}{9}-2p\pm\frac{p^2-1}{3}}{2}=\frac{p^2-2p}{3} \text{ or } -\frac{1-2p}{3} \end{eqnarray*}
To make these integers we pick the first set of solutions if  $p\equiv 1 \text{ (mod 3)}$ , the second set otherwise.

