<details><summary>Solution - grinding Russian Combinatorics</summary>$$ \color{red} \spadesuit\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Initial Assumptions.}}\color{red} \spadesuit $$  Without loss of generality, let the  $3$  colors used be <span style="color:#2A52BE">cerulean</span>, <span style="color:#5F9EA0">gray</span>, and <span style="color:#00FFFF">cyan</span>. Since every color is used at least once, it goes without saying that there exists a line parallel to the  $y=0$  line with at least  $2$  points of distinct colors; without loss of generality, we would apply a translation that sends said line to  $y=0$  (note that this does not really have any effect on the solution, it just allows us to work more nicely with said line). Finally, without loss of generality, assume that the two colors present on this line are <span style="color:#2A52BE">cerulean</span> and <span style="color:#5F9EA0">gray</span>.
Let  $\mathcal{C}$  denote the set of all <span style="color:#00FFFF">cyan</span> points in plane, and let  $l_0$  be the line  $y=0$ . We split our solution in two cases:   $$ \color{blue} \clubsuit\color{blue} \boxed{\textbf{ $\mathcal{C} \cap l_0 \equiv \emptyset$ .}}\color{blue} \clubsuit $$  In this case, let  $P$  be a <span style="color:#00FFFF">cyan</span> point - we can then consider the projection of  $P$  on  $l_0$  (so the point with coordinates  $(x_P, 0)$ ) - it is necessarily not <span style="color:#00FFFF">cyan</span>, so it is, without a loss of generality, <span style="color:#5F9EA0">gray</span> in color, and all we need to do is consider any point on  $l_0$  that is <span style="color:#2A52BE">cerulean</span> - since such point does indeed exist, the problem condition immediately follows.
[asy]
