Let  $T$  be a point on  $\odot (ABC)$  such that  $AT||BC$ .It is well known that  $X,D,T$  are collinear.Hence,  $$ \angle AXD=\angle AXT=\angle B-\angle C=\angle AZP=\frac{1}{2} \angle AZD $$  Hence  $Z$  is the center of the circle  $\odot(ADX)$ Next we will prove that  $Y$  lies on this circle.Using power of  $Z$  wrt  $\odot(DNPM)$  we get,  $$ pow(Z,\odot(DNPM))=ZM\times ZP=ZA^2=ZD^2 $$  SO  $ZD$  is tangent to  $\odot(DNPM)$ .Also observe that,( $S$  is mid point of  $MN$ )  $$ (M,P;Y,D)=N(M,P;S,P_{\infty})=-1 $$  So  $ZY$  is also tangent to  $\odot(DNPM)$  which shows  $ZD=ZY$ .The claim is proved.


Now since  $ZA^2=ZM\times ZP$   so  $Z$  lies on the radical axis of  $\odot(ABC)$  and  $\odot(DNPM)$ .So the perpendicular from Z to the line joining the center of this 2 circle (which becomes the Euler line of ABC) is radical axis of  $\odot(ABC)$  and  $\odot(DNPM)$ .
So the problem is proved using the radical axis theorem on  $\odot(ABC),\odot(DNPM),\odot(AYDX)$ . $\blacksquare$

