For part (a), we use complex numbers. Toss the configuration onto the complex plane and let the small alphabets of the respective points denote their affixes.

We fix  $a$  and  $b$  and model  $c$  w.r.t. them with the given condition.


Note that the midpoint of  $BC$  is  $\dfrac{b+c}{2}$ . Now the midpoint of  $BM$  is  $\dfrac{b + \dfrac{b+c}{2}}{2}=\dfrac{3b+c}{4}$ .


Now note that since  $AB=AM$ , the foot of the perpendicular from  $A$  to  $BC$  coincides with the midpoint of  $BM$ . Thus  $\dfrac{3b+c}{4}=\dfrac{1}{2}(b + c + a - bc\overline{a})$  which gives,
\begin{align*}
    &\dfrac{3b+c}{2}=\dfrac{ab+ac+a^2-bc}{a}
    \implies &3ab + ac = 2ab + 2ac + 2a^2 -2bc
    \implies &ab = ac + ac + 2a^2 - 2bc\implies a(b-2a) = c(a-2b)
.\end{align*}
Thus  $c = \dfrac{a(b-2a)}{a-2b}$ . Note that  $a-2b\neq 0$  because otherwise, we get  $a = 2b$  which gives a contradiction that  $1=2$  when we take modulo on both sides.


Now note that  $h = a + b + c = a + b+ \dfrac{a(b-2a)}{a-2b}$  and thus,
\begin{align*}
    n&=\dfrac{h+a}{2} = \dfrac{2a + b+ \dfrac{a(b-2a)}{a-2b}}{2}
    &=\dfrac{(2a+b)(a-2b) + (ab-2a^2)}{2(a-2b)}
    &=\dfrac{2a^2 + ab - 4ab - 2b^2 - 2a^2 + ab}{2(a-2b)}
    &=\dfrac{-2ab-2b^2}{2(a-2b)}
    &=-\dfrac{b(a+b)}{a-2b}
.\end{align*}


Now,
\[m=\dfrac{b+c}{2}=\dfrac{b+\dfrac{a(b-2a)}{a-2b}}{2}=\dfrac{ab-2b^2+ab-2a^2}{2(a-2b)}=\dfrac{ab-a^2-b^2}{a-2b}. \]
Further note that  $AH$  is the  $A$ -median of  $\triangle ABM$  due to isosceles condition. Now since  $I$  is the intersection of  $AH$  and  $B$ -midline, it becomes the centroid of  $\triangle ABM$ . Thus,
\begin{align*}
    i&=\dfrac{a+b+m}{3}=\dfrac{a+b+\dfrac{ab-a^2-b^2}{a-2b}}{3}
    &=\dfrac{(a+b)(a-2b)+ab-a^2-b^2}{3(a-2b)}
    &=\dfrac{a^2 - 2ab + ab -2b^2 + ab - a^2 - b^2}{3(a-2b)}
    &=\dfrac{-b^2}{a-2b}
.\end{align*}


Now to finish, we just need to show that  $|i-o|=|i-n|\iff \left|\dfrac{-b^2}{a-2b}-0\right|=\left|\dfrac{-b^2}{a-2b} - \left(-\dfrac{b(a+b)}{a-2b}\right)\right|$ .


Now we have that,
\[ \left|\dfrac{-b^2}{a-2b} - \left(-\dfrac{b(a+b)}{a-2b}\right)\right| =\left|\dfrac{b(a+b)}{a-2b} - \dfrac{b^2}{a-2b}\right| = \left|\dfrac{ba+b^2-b^2}{a-2b}\right|=\left|\dfrac{ba}{a-2b}\right|. \]

Thus to finish, we just need to show,
\[ \left|\dfrac{-b^2}{a-2b}\right| = \left|\dfrac{ba}{a-2b}\right|\iff \dfrac{|b|^2}{|a-2b|}=\dfrac{|ab|}{|a-2b|}\iff\dfrac{1}{|a-2b|} = \dfrac{1}{|a-2b|} \]
which is indeed true and we are done.


Now we move onto part (b).

Let  $X$  denote the intersection of  $\odot(AH)$  and  $AM$ . Note that  $X$  is the  $A$ -humpty point.

Now note that since  $\triangle ABM$  is isosceles, due to reflection over  $AH$ , we get that  $AB\leftrightarrow AM$  and that  $\odot(AH)\leftrightarrow\odot(AH)$ . Thus  $X\leftrightarrow K$ .

Now note that  $\measuredangle AKH=90^\circ$ . This gives us that  $K$  is the foot of the altitude from  $C$  in  $\triangle ABC$  and thus  $\overline{C-H-K}$  are collinear.

Now from EGMO Lemma 1.44, we get that  $MK$  is tangent to  $\odot(AH)$ . Thus after reflection over  $AH$ , we get that  $BX$  is also tangent to  $\odot(ABM)$ .

Now again due to reflection, we get that  $\operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(AH)}(B)=\operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(AH)}(M)$  since the line  $AH$  passes through the center of the circle  $\odot(AH)$ .

It is well known that  $MB^2 = MX\cdot MA$ . But then the RHS is just  $\operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(AH)}(M)$  from which we get  $BM^2=BK\cdot BA$ . Further, due to the tangency we get  $BK\cdot BA = BX^2$  which gives us  $BM^2=BX^2\implies BM = BX$ . Thus  $X$  lies on  $\odot(B)$ .

Now note that it is well known that the reflection of the  $A$ -humpty point lies on the  $A$ -symmedian. Clearly  $X'$  lies on  $\odot(B)$ .

Now let  $P=AX'\cap \odot(B)$ . We first show that  $ANPX$  is cyclic. We have,
\begin{align*}
    \measuredangle ANX&=2\measuredangle AKX=2\measuredangle ABM
    &=2\measuredangle BMX
    &=\measuredangle BMX+\measuredangle X'MB
    &=\measuredangle X'MX=\measuredangle X'PX=\measuredangle APX
.\end{align*}
Thus we now have  $ANPX$  is cyclic. To finish, note that,
\begin{align*}
    \measuredangle ANX&=\measuredangle NAX
    &=\measuredangle HAM
    &=\measuredangle BAH=\measuredangle BAN
.\end{align*}

This finally means that  $AB$  is tangent to  $\odot(ANPX)$  and we are done.



