*Solution:* Define  $f(x) = \sin x - \frac{2}{\pi}x$ . We need to show that  $f>0$  in  $[0,\pi/2]$ . The inequality is trivially true at  $0$  and  $\pi/2$ . Evaluating the first and second derivatives of  $f$  we get
    \begin{align*}
        f'(x)  & = \cos x - \frac{2}{\pi} 
        f''(x) & = -\sin x.
    \end{align*}
Now, just observe that  $f'$  is  $0$  at  $x = \arccos\left( \frac{2}{\pi} \right)$ . Finally note that  $f'(x) \ge 0$  for  $x \in \left[0,\arccos\left( \frac{2}{\pi} \right)\right]$  and  $f'(x) < 0$  for  $x \in \left[\arccos\left( \frac{2}{\pi} \right), \frac{\pi}{2}\right]$ . This means  $f$  will start decreasing after a point. Here's a precise graph of  $f$ .
[asy]
