Fix  $\omega$  and  $i$ , consider all the combination  $\omega+i$ ,  $\omega-i$ ,  $\overline{\omega}+i$  and  $\overline{\omega}-i$ , the polynomial having them as root gives the desired polynomial. In particular, we have 
\begin{align*}
[x- (\omega+i)][x-(\omega-i)][x - (\overline{\omega}+i)][x - (\overline{\omega}-i)] & = [x^2 -2\omega x + \omega^2+1 ] [x^2 -2\overline{\omega} x+ \overline{\omega}^2+1 ] 
& = [x^2 -2\omega x - \omega][x^2 -2\overline{\omega} x- \overline{\omega}] 
& = x^4 -2(\omega +\overline{\omega})x^3 + (4 \omega \overline{\omega} - \omega -\overline{\omega} ) x^2  +4 \omega \overline{\omega} x +\omega \overline{\omega} 
\end{align*} 
Note that  $\omega +\overline{\omega}=-1$  and  $\omega \overline{\omega}  = 1$ , the desired polynomial is  $x^4+2x^3+5x^2+4x+1$ P.S. Indeed the desired polynomial is the resultant of the two given polynomials, which has deep connection to extension of fields in Galois theory.

