solution_md:
 $\textbf{(a):}$  For sum of elements in row to be even one must have  $(1,1), (0,0)$  as the only options to fill in row $_{1}$  and row $_{2}$  , Now suppose sum of elemts till  $2n-2$  cells is  $\mathcal{S}$ .

if  $\mathcal{S} \equiv \pmod{2}$  then in  $C_{1n}$   $0$  is the only possibility, and if  $\mathcal{S} \equiv 1 \pmod{2}$  then in  $C_{2n}$   $1$   is only possibility. So Number of ways is simply the number of ways to form  $\mathcal{S}$  or to arrange  $(1,1)$  ,  $(0,0)$  in  $n-1$  spaces that can be done is  $2^{n-1}$  ways , so answer for this part is  $\boxed{2^{n-1}}$  $\textbf{(b):}$  Suppose  $\mathcal{B}(n)$  are the total number of ways so we have \[
  \mathcal{B}(n) = \left.
  \begin{cases}
    2^{n-1}, & \text{for } n \equiv 0 \pmod 2 
    0, & \text{for } n \equiv 1 \pmod 2 
    \end{cases}
  \right\}
\] $\emph{Proof:-}$  Suppose  $n$  is off then , for sum of rows odd only  $(1,0)$  or  $(0,1)$  are permissible.

Now suppose  $t$   $1's$  are there in Column  $1$  so  $n-j$  are  $0$   and we must have  $j$  to be odd , then  $n-j$  is even. Now in Column  $2$  there would be  $n-j$  many  $1's$  giving sum of elements in Column  $2$  even which is not possible , hence in this case  $\mathcal{B}(n)=0$ Now if  $n$  is even then we need to choose any odd number of cells to fill  $1$  in Column  $1$  as a result that will fix the Column  $2$  numbers also , that can be done in  $\binom{n}{1}+\binom{n}{3}+\cdots+\binom{n}{n-1}=2^{n-1}$  ways , hence:
\[
\mathcal{B}(n) = \left.
\begin{cases}
2^{n-1}, & \text{for } n \equiv 0 \pmod 2 
0, & \text{for } n \equiv 1 \pmod 2 
\end{cases}
\right\}
\]  $\square$ .

