*Solution:*  The answer is  $(t,c) = \left(\frac{4}{3}, \frac{3}{4} \right)$ . Call the given limit to  $\mathcal{L}$ . Since  $f(x) \coloneqq x^{\frac{1}{3}}$  is continuous, it must be Riemann integrable. By Riemann Sums we can conclude that 
\[ \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{r = 1}^{n} f\left(\frac{r}{n}\right) = \int_{0}^{1} f(t) \, dt. \]
From the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, we can now conclude that
\[ \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{n^\frac{4}{3}} \sum_{r = 1}^{n} r^\frac{1}{3} = \frac{x^{\frac{4}{3}}}{\frac{4}{3}}\Bigg|_{0}^{1} = \frac{3}{4}.\]
Observe that 
\[\mathcal{L} = \lim_{n \to \infty} \left( n^{\frac{4}{3} - t}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{\sum_{r = 1}^{n} r^{\frac{1}{3}}}{n^\frac{4}{3}}\right). \]
Since the second parenthesis is convergent, so must be the first one. But we know that 
\begin{align*}
\lim_{n \to \infty} n^{\frac{4}{3} - t} = 
\begin{cases}
0 \qquad &\text{if  $t> \frac{4}{3}$ ,} 
1 \qquad &\text{if  $ t = \frac{4}{3}$ ,} 
+\infty \qquad &\text{if  $t < \frac{4}{3}$ .}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
Only the second case is valid otherwise we get a contradiction on the condition  $c > 0$  or on the existence of  $\mathcal{L}$ .  $\mathcal{L}$  can be simply evaluated as  $3/4$  by previous calculations. This completes the solution.  $\blacksquare$

