    We can define  $g'(x)$  piecewise as:
      $$ g(x) = \begin{cases}
        f'(x)\sin\left(\frac 1x\right) - \frac{f(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x^2}~ &\text{if} ~ x \neq 0 
        0 &\text{if} ~ x = 0.
    \end{cases} $$      This implies  $\lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\sin\left(\frac 1x\right) - \frac{f(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x^2} = 0$     \begin{align*}
        \implies & \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\sin\left(\frac 1x\right) & = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{f(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x^2}
        \implies & \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\sin\left(\frac 1x\right) & = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{f(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x^2}
        \implies & \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\sin\left(\frac 1x\right) & = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{f'(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)+\frac{f(x)\sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x^2}}{2x}
        \implies & \lim_{x \to 0} 2xf'(x)\sin\left(\frac 1x\right) & = \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)+\frac{f(x)\sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x^2}
        \implies & 0 & = \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)+f(x)\frac{sin\left( \frac{1}{x} \right)}{x^2}
        \implies & 0 & = \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x)\cos\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)+0
        \implies \lim_{x \to 0} f'(x) = 0
        \implies f'(0) = 0
    \end{align*}
    Note that we are able to take  $\lim_{x \to 0} f(x)\frac{\sin\left( \frac{1}{x} \right)}{x^2} = 0$  as if it exists, it must be zero as  $\sin\left(\frac{1}{x} \right)$  is oscilating.
