By change of base we find that   $$ \frac{\ln a}{\ln x}+\frac{\ln a}{\ln y} = 4 \frac{\ln a}{\ln xy} \implies \frac{\ln x + \ln y}{\ln(x)\ln(y)}=\frac{4}{\ln x+\ln y}. $$   Letting  $a=\ln x$  and  $b = \ln y$ , we have
\begin{align*}
(a+b)^2=4ab \implies a^2+b^2-2ab=0 \implies (a-b)^2=0.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we must have  $\ln x = \ln y$ , further implying that  $x=y$ .  $\square$
